---
title: Table State (Octane) Guide
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useTable"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External Atoms"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External State"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-subscribe","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Subscribe"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"table-state-octane-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State (Octane) Guide"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"do-you-need-to-manage-external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you need to Manage External State?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", or any of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Octane, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"state-in-v9","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State in v9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"alien-signals"},{"type":"text","value":" implementation and supports performant derived state. For TanStack Table, this means the table can derive a full state store from independent state atoms and Octane can subscribe to only the pieces of table state that a component actually needs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A table instance has a few state surfaces:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.baseAtoms"},{"type":"text","value":" are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" together."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" is the Octane adapter's selected state. Its type and value come from the selector passed as the second argument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The important change from previous versions is that table state is now atomic. Octane can subscribe to all selected state, a selected subset of state, or a single atom such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.rowSelection"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"feature-based-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature-based State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State slices are only created for the features that are registered in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":". This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, if "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" includes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPaginationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":", TypeScript exposes pagination state APIs and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":". If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" does not include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPaginationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" should not be available in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same feature-based typing applies to built-in features and custom feature-provided state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"accessing-table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing Table State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are two different questions when reading table state:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you only need the current value?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Or should this Octane component re-render when that value changes?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use a selector subscription for reactive rendering."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"reading-state-without-subscribing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading State Without Subscribing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also read the current flat store snapshot:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These reads are not Octane subscriptions. Calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" during render reads the current value, but future changes will not automatically re-render that component unless something else causes a render. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" state selection, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":", or even a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" hook from TanStack Store."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"reading-reactive-state-with-usetable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading Reactive State with useTable"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second argument to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" is a TanStack Store selector. By default, the selector effectively selects all registered table state, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" contains the full state and the component re-renders when any selected state changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can pass your own selector to make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" contain only the reactive state values that you want to cause re-renders. The Octane adapter compares selected values shallowly. The default selector selects all registered table state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For large tables, you can also opt the parent table component out of table-state re-renders and subscribe lower in the tree:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With this pattern, the parent component will not re-render for table state changes. Put reactive reads inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" where the UI actually needs them."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"optimizing-re-renders-with-selectors-and-table-subscribe","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimizing Re-renders with Selectors and table.Subscribe"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" when you want table-state re-renders to happen at a specific place in the Octane tree. This is useful for large or expensive tables, but it is usually something to reach for after the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" selector becomes a visible performance issue."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Without a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" prop, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" subscribes to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" and requires a selector. With a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" prop, it can subscribe directly to one atom or store, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.rowSelection"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)\n\n<table.Subscribe\n  selector={(state) => ({\n    columnFilters: state.columnFilters,\n    globalFilter: state.globalFilter,\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  })}\n>\n  {() => <SubscribedRows table={table} />}\n</table.Subscribe>\n\nfunction SubscribedRows({ table }) @{\n  <tbody>\n    @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n      <tr>...</tr>\n    }\n  </tbody>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also subscribe directly to a single atom and select one value from it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<table.Subscribe\n  source={table.atoms.rowSelection}\n  selector={(rowSelection) => rowSelection[row.id]}\n>\n  {(isSelected) => (\n    <input\n      type=\"checkbox\"\n      checked={!!isSelected}\n      onClick={row.getToggleSelectedHandler()}\n    />\n  )}\n</table.Subscribe>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Advanced subscription patterns require understanding which table APIs depend on which state slices. For example, a row model may depend on filtering, sorting, and pagination, while one row selection checkbox may only need one row's selection value. Start with the default selector, then optimize with selectors and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" where render cost matters."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"setting-table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Setting Table State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes because they preserve the feature's own rules and related updates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For example, use pagination APIs instead of mutating pagination state yourself:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same idea applies across features. Use APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnFilters(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleVisibility()"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.toggleSelected()"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of manually editing the underlying state object."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you only care about setting starting values, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", write to that external atom instead; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-initial-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Initial State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". You still do not need to manage that state yourself."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetSorting()"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetPagination()"},{"type":"text","value":". Changing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" object later does not reset table state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", prefer exactly one of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":" as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":"; external "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" syncs into the table's internal base atom."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"resetting-to-initial-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting to Initial State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature reset APIs reset to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" by default. Many reset APIs also accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Slice reset APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resetPagination()"},{"type":"text","value":" update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"controlled-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this because they preserve the atomic state model and let Octane subscribe to exactly the slices it needs."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External Atoms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCreateAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" from TanStack Store, pass them to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option, and subscribe to them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" anywhere else in your app."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is especially useful for server-side data fetching. Pagination, sorting, or filters often belong in a query key, and external atoms let the app and the table share those values without lifting the entire table state through Octane state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'octane'\nimport {\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  // no paginatedRowModel: manual server-side pagination does not need it\n})\n\nfunction App() @{\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const [result, setResult] = useState({ rows: [], rowCount: 0 })\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let cancelled = false\n    fetchData(pagination).then((nextResult) => {\n      if (!cancelled) setResult(nextResult)\n    })\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true\n    }\n  }, [pagination])\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: result.rows,\n    rowCount: result.rowCount,\n    atoms: {\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n    manualPagination: true,\n  })\n\n  // table pagination APIs update paginationAtom\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" option. For example, if you pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms.pagination"},{"type":"text","value":", table pagination APIs update that atom directly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The classic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Octane state updates re-render the owner component, and that render cannot be avoided by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" selector in the same way atom subscriptions can be placed lower in the tree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To control a slice with external state, pass both the state value and the matching callback:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStateChange"},{"type":"text","value":" option (a single global state callback) is gone in v9. It is not supported by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":". Use per-slice "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks paired with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.<slice>"},{"type":"text","value":", or external atoms via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. If you truly need to observe every state change, subscribe to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" directly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"render-and-commit-timing","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render and Commit Timing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"During an Octane render, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" stages the newest options immediately, so table reads in that same render see fresh data, columns, callbacks, and controlled state. It does not publish that controlled state to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":" while the component is rendering."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After Octane accepts the render, a layout effect marks its snapshot committed and publishes the captured controlled state. Suspended or abandoned renders therefore cannot leak speculative state. The owner drops the matching post-commit notification because it has already rendered that snapshot, while independently mounted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" consumers still receive the update before paint."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This commit behavior applies to controlled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" options. External atoms remain the direct source of truth and table APIs write to them synchronously."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":5,"id":"on-state-change-callbacks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On State Change Callbacks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" option. They work like Octane state setters: an updater can be a raw value or a function that receives the previous value and returns the next value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you provide an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" callback, also provide the corresponding value in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":". For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSortingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" should be paired with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to add logic inside a callback, check whether the incoming updater is a function or a value:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n    setPagination((old) => {\n      const next = updater instanceof Function ? updater(old) : updater\n\n      // side effects or validation can happen here\n\n      return next\n    })\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"state-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use. This is useful for Octane state, external atoms, and helper functions that work with table state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableState<typeof features>"},{"type":"text","value":" is inferred from the features registered on that table:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n})\n\ntype MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer feature-specific state types like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortingState"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PaginationState"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RowSelectionState"},{"type":"text","value":" when you are creating local state or external atoms for one slice."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"table-state-octane-guide","text":"Table State (Octane) Guide","level":2},{"id":"do-you-need-to-manage-external-state","text":"Do you need to Manage External State?","level":3},{"id":"state-in-v9","text":"State in v9","level":3},{"id":"feature-based-state","text":"Feature-based State","level":3},{"id":"accessing-table-state","text":"Accessing Table State","level":3},{"id":"reading-state-without-subscribing","text":"Reading State Without Subscribing","level":4},{"id":"reading-reactive-state-with-usetable","text":"Reading Reactive State with useTable","level":4},{"id":"optimizing-re-renders-with-selectors-and-table-subscribe","text":"Optimizing Re-renders with Selectors and table.Subscribe","level":4},{"id":"setting-table-state","text":"Setting Table State","level":3},{"id":"custom-initial-state","text":"Custom Initial State","level":3},{"id":"resetting-to-initial-state","text":"Resetting to Initial State","level":4},{"id":"controlled-state","text":"Controlled State","level":3},{"id":"external-atoms","text":"External Atoms","level":4},{"id":"external-state","text":"External State","level":4},{"id":"render-and-commit-timing","text":"Render and Commit Timing","level":4},{"id":"on-state-change-callbacks","text":"On State Change Callbacks","level":5},{"id":"state-types","text":"State Types","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:\n\n- [Basic useTable](../examples/basic-use-table)\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms)\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state)\n- [Basic Subscribe](../examples/basic-subscribe)\n\n## Table State (Octane) Guide\n\n> **If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states.**\n\nUnderstanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results.\n\n### Do you need to Manage External State?\n\nYou usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to `initialState`, `atoms`, `state`, or any of the `on[State]Change` table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally.\n\nThere will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Octane, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state.\n\n### State in v9\n\nTanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the `alien-signals` implementation and supports performant derived state. For TanStack Table, this means the table can derive a full state store from independent state atoms and Octane can subscribe to only the pieces of table state that a component actually needs.\n\nA table instance has a few state surfaces:\n\n- `table.baseAtoms` are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state.\n- `table.atoms` are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice.\n- `table.store` is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered `table.atoms` together.\n- `table.state` is the Octane adapter's selected state. Its type and value come from the selector passed as the second argument to `useTable`.\n\nThe important change from previous versions is that table state is now atomic. Octane can subscribe to all selected state, a selected subset of state, or a single atom such as `table.atoms.rowSelection`.\n\n### Feature-based State\n\nState slices are only created for the features that are registered in `features`. This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference.\n\nFor example, if `features` includes `rowPaginationFeature`, TypeScript exposes pagination state APIs and `table.atoms.pagination`. If `features` does not include `rowPaginationFeature`, `pagination` should not be available in `table.atoms`, `table.store.state`, `table.state`, `initialState`, `state`, or `atoms`.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered\n```\n\nThe same feature-based typing applies to built-in features and custom feature-provided state.\n\n### Accessing Table State\n\nThere are two different questions when reading table state:\n\n- Do you only need the current value?\n- Or should this Octane component re-render when that value changes?\n\nUse a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use a selector subscription for reactive rendering.\n\n#### Reading State Without Subscribing\n\nThe simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:\n\n```tsx\nconst pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\nYou can also read the current flat store snapshot:\n\n```tsx\nconst tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination\n```\n\nThese reads are not Octane subscriptions. Calling `table.atoms.pagination.get()` or `table.store.state.pagination` during render reads the current value, but future changes will not automatically re-render that component unless something else causes a render. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use `useTable` state selection, `table.Subscribe`, or even a `useSelector` hook from TanStack Store.\n\n#### Reading Reactive State with useTable\n\nThe second argument to `useTable` is a TanStack Store selector. By default, the selector effectively selects all registered table state, so `table.state` contains the full state and the component re-renders when any selected state changes.\n\nYou can pass your own selector to make `table.state` contain only the reactive state values that you want to cause re-renders. The Octane adapter compares selected values shallowly. The default selector selects all registered table state.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination\n```\n\nFor large tables, you can also opt the parent table component out of table-state re-renders and subscribe lower in the tree:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)\n```\n\nWith this pattern, the parent component will not re-render for table state changes. Put reactive reads inside `table.Subscribe` where the UI actually needs them.\n\n#### Optimizing Re-renders with Selectors and table.Subscribe\n\nUse `table.Subscribe` when you want table-state re-renders to happen at a specific place in the Octane tree. This is useful for large or expensive tables, but it is usually something to reach for after the default `useTable` selector becomes a visible performance issue.\n\nWithout a `source` prop, `table.Subscribe` subscribes to `table.store` and requires a selector. With a `source` prop, it can subscribe directly to one atom or store, such as `table.atoms.rowSelection`.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)\n\n<table.Subscribe\n  selector={(state) => ({\n    columnFilters: state.columnFilters,\n    globalFilter: state.globalFilter,\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  })}\n>\n  {() => <SubscribedRows table={table} />}\n</table.Subscribe>\n\nfunction SubscribedRows({ table }) @{\n  <tbody>\n    @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n      <tr>...</tr>\n    }\n  </tbody>\n}\n```\n\nYou can also subscribe directly to a single atom and select one value from it:\n\n```tsx\n<table.Subscribe\n  source={table.atoms.rowSelection}\n  selector={(rowSelection) => rowSelection[row.id]}\n>\n  {(isSelected) => (\n    <input\n      type=\"checkbox\"\n      checked={!!isSelected}\n      onClick={row.getToggleSelectedHandler()}\n    />\n  )}\n</table.Subscribe>\n```\n\nAdvanced subscription patterns require understanding which table APIs depend on which state slices. For example, a row model may depend on filtering, sorting, and pagination, while one row selection checkbox may only need one row's selection value. Start with the default selector, then optimize with selectors and `table.Subscribe` where render cost matters.\n\n### Setting Table State\n\nYou should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes because they preserve the feature's own rules and related updates.\n\nFor example, use pagination APIs instead of mutating pagination state yourself:\n\n```tsx\ntable.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)\n```\n\nThe same idea applies across features. Use APIs like `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setColumnFilters(...)`, `column.toggleVisibility()`, or `row.toggleSelected()` instead of manually editing the underlying state object.\n\nIf you only care about setting starting values, use `initialState`. If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API.\n\nIf you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:\n\n```tsx\ntable.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))\n```\n\nDirect base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through `atoms`, write to that external atom instead; `table.atoms.pagination` will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom.\n\n### Custom Initial State\n\nIf you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use `initialState`. You still do not need to manage that state yourself.\n\n`initialState` only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as `table.resetSorting()` or `table.resetPagination()`. Changing the `initialState` object later does not reset table state.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like `pagination`, prefer exactly one of `initialState.pagination`, `atoms.pagination`, or `state.pagination` as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external `state`; external `state` syncs into the table's internal base atom.\n\n#### Resetting to Initial State\n\nFeature reset APIs reset to `table.initialState` by default. Many reset APIs also accept `true` to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:\n\n```tsx\ntable.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)\n```\n\nSlice reset APIs like `resetPagination()` update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core `table.reset()` API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nIf you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this because they preserve the atomic state model and let Octane subscribe to exactly the slices it needs.\n\n#### External Atoms\n\nUse external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with `useCreateAtom` from TanStack Store, pass them to the table's `atoms` option, and subscribe to them with `useSelector` anywhere else in your app.\n\nThis is especially useful for server-side data fetching. Pagination, sorting, or filters often belong in a query key, and external atoms let the app and the table share those values without lifting the entire table state through Octane state.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'octane'\nimport {\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  // no paginatedRowModel: manual server-side pagination does not need it\n})\n\nfunction App() @{\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const [result, setResult] = useState({ rows: [], rowCount: 0 })\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let cancelled = false\n    fetchData(pagination).then((nextResult) => {\n      if (!cancelled) setResult(nextResult)\n    })\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true\n    }\n  }, [pagination])\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: result.rows,\n    rowCount: result.rowCount,\n    atoms: {\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n    manualPagination: true,\n  })\n\n  // table pagination APIs update paginationAtom\n}\n```\n\nWhen using the `atoms` option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching `on[State]Change` option. For example, if you pass `atoms.pagination`, table pagination APIs update that atom directly.\n\n#### External State\n\nThe classic `state` plus `on[State]Change` pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Octane state updates re-render the owner component, and that render cannot be avoided by the `useTable` selector in the same way atom subscriptions can be placed lower in the tree.\n\nTo control a slice with external state, pass both the state value and the matching callback:\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})\n```\n\nThe v8-style `onStateChange` option (a single global state callback) is gone in v9. It is not supported by `useTable`. Use per-slice `on[State]Change` callbacks paired with `state.<slice>`, or external atoms via the `atoms` option. If you truly need to observe every state change, subscribe to `table.store` directly.\n\n#### Render and Commit Timing\n\nDuring an Octane render, `useTable` stages the newest options immediately, so table reads in that same render see fresh data, columns, callbacks, and controlled state. It does not publish that controlled state to `table.store` while the component is rendering.\n\nAfter Octane accepts the render, a layout effect marks its snapshot committed and publishes the captured controlled state. Suspended or abandoned renders therefore cannot leak speculative state. The owner drops the matching post-commit notification because it has already rendered that snapshot, while independently mounted `table.Subscribe` consumers still receive the update before paint.\n\nThis commit behavior applies to controlled `state` options. External atoms remain the direct source of truth and table APIs write to them synchronously.\n\n##### On State Change Callbacks\n\nThe `on[State]Change` callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the `state` option. They work like Octane state setters: an updater can be a raw value or a function that receives the previous value and returns the next value.\n\nIf you provide an `on[State]Change` callback, also provide the corresponding value in `state`. For example, `onSortingChange` should be paired with `state.sorting`.\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n})\n```\n\nIf you need to add logic inside a callback, check whether the incoming updater is a function or a value:\n\n```tsx\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n    setPagination((old) => {\n      const next = updater instanceof Function ? updater(old) : updater\n\n      // side effects or validation can happen here\n\n      return next\n    })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### State Types\n\nMost complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use. This is useful for Octane state, external atoms, and helper functions that work with table state.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n])\n```\n\n`TableState<typeof features>` is inferred from the features registered on that table:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n})\n\ntype MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>\n```\n\nPrefer feature-specific state types like `SortingState`, `PaginationState`, or `RowSelectionState` when you are creating local state or external atoms for one slice.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Table State (Octane) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these examples:\n\n- [Basic useTable](../examples/basic-use-table)\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms)\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state)\n- [Basic Subscribe](../examples/basic-subscribe)\n\n## Table State (Octane) Guide\n\n> **If you boil TanStack Table down to one sentence: TanStack Table is a large state-management coordinator for table states.**\n\nUnderstanding this guide is fundamental to understanding how TanStack Table works and how to interact with it for the best results.\n\n### Do you need to Manage External State?\n\nYou usually do NOT need to manage table state yourself. If you pass nothing to `initialState`, `atoms`, `state`, or any of the `on[State]Change` table options, TanStack Table will manage its own state internally.\n\nThere will be situations where you need to customize how you interact with the internal table state, or even hoist it up to your own scopes. TanStack Table lets you read, subscribe to, or own the state slices that matter to your app. This guide explains how table state works in Octane, how to read it, and when to use external atoms or external state.\n\n### State in v9\n\nTanStack Table v9 overhauled state management around TanStack Store. TanStack Store uses the `alien-signals` implementation and supports performant derived state. For TanStack Table, this means the table can derive a full state store from independent state atoms and Octane can subscribe to only the pieces of table state that a component actually needs.\n\nA table instance has a few state surfaces:\n\n- `table.baseAtoms` are the internal writable atoms created from the resolved initial state.\n- `table.atoms` are readonly derived atoms exposed per registered state slice.\n- `table.store` is a readonly flat TanStack Store derived by putting all of the registered `table.atoms` together.\n- `table.state` is the Octane adapter's selected state. Its type and value come from the selector passed as the second argument to `useTable`.\n\nThe important change from previous versions is that table state is now atomic. Octane can subscribe to all selected state, a selected subset of state, or a single atom such as `table.atoms.rowSelection`.\n\n### Feature-based State\n\nState slices are only created for the features that are registered in `features`. This keeps TanStack Table tree-shakeable and gives TypeScript more accurate state inference.\n\nFor example, if `features` includes `rowPaginationFeature`, TypeScript exposes pagination state APIs and `table.atoms.pagination`. If `features` does not include `rowPaginationFeature`, `pagination` should not be available in `table.atoms`, `table.store.state`, `table.state`, `initialState`, `state`, or `atoms`.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\ntable.atoms.pagination.get()\ntable.atoms.sorting.get()\n\n// table.atoms.rowSelection // TypeScript error unless rowSelectionFeature is registered\n```\n\nThe same feature-based typing applies to built-in features and custom feature-provided state.\n\n### Accessing Table State\n\nThere are two different questions when reading table state:\n\n- Do you only need the current value?\n- Or should this Octane component re-render when that value changes?\n\nUse a direct atom or store read for the current value. Use a selector subscription for reactive rendering.\n\n#### Reading State Without Subscribing\n\nThe simplest and most performant way to read a current state value is to read the matching atom:\n\n```tsx\nconst pagination = table.atoms.pagination.get()\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\nYou can also read the current flat store snapshot:\n\n```tsx\nconst tableState = table.store.state\nconst pagination = table.store.state.pagination\n```\n\nThese reads are not Octane subscriptions. Calling `table.atoms.pagination.get()` or `table.store.state.pagination` during render reads the current value, but future changes will not automatically re-render that component unless something else causes a render. If the UI needs to stay reactive to table state changes, use `useTable` state selection, `table.Subscribe`, or even a `useSelector` hook from TanStack Store.\n\n#### Reading Reactive State with useTable\n\nThe second argument to `useTable` is a TanStack Store selector. By default, the selector effectively selects all registered table state, so `table.state` contains the full state and the component re-renders when any selected state changes.\n\nYou can pass your own selector to make `table.state` contain only the reactive state values that you want to cause re-renders. The Octane adapter compares selected values shallowly. The default selector selects all registered table state.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  (state) => ({\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  }),\n)\n\ntable.state.pagination\n```\n\nFor large tables, you can also opt the parent table component out of table-state re-renders and subscribe lower in the tree:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)\n```\n\nWith this pattern, the parent component will not re-render for table state changes. Put reactive reads inside `table.Subscribe` where the UI actually needs them.\n\n#### Optimizing Re-renders with Selectors and table.Subscribe\n\nUse `table.Subscribe` when you want table-state re-renders to happen at a specific place in the Octane tree. This is useful for large or expensive tables, but it is usually something to reach for after the default `useTable` selector becomes a visible performance issue.\n\nWithout a `source` prop, `table.Subscribe` subscribes to `table.store` and requires a selector. With a `source` prop, it can subscribe directly to one atom or store, such as `table.atoms.rowSelection`.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable(\n  {\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n  },\n  () => null,\n)\n\n<table.Subscribe\n  selector={(state) => ({\n    columnFilters: state.columnFilters,\n    globalFilter: state.globalFilter,\n    pagination: state.pagination,\n  })}\n>\n  {() => <SubscribedRows table={table} />}\n</table.Subscribe>\n\nfunction SubscribedRows({ table }) @{\n  <tbody>\n    @for (const row of table.getRowModel().rows; key row.id) {\n      <tr>...</tr>\n    }\n  </tbody>\n}\n```\n\nYou can also subscribe directly to a single atom and select one value from it:\n\n```tsx\n<table.Subscribe\n  source={table.atoms.rowSelection}\n  selector={(rowSelection) => rowSelection[row.id]}\n>\n  {(isSelected) => (\n    <input\n      type=\"checkbox\"\n      checked={!!isSelected}\n      onClick={row.getToggleSelectedHandler()}\n    />\n  )}\n</table.Subscribe>\n```\n\nAdvanced subscription patterns require understanding which table APIs depend on which state slices. For example, a row model may depend on filtering, sorting, and pagination, while one row selection checkbox may only need one row's selection value. Start with the default selector, then optimize with selectors and `table.Subscribe` where render cost matters.\n\n### Setting Table State\n\nYou should almost never need to set table state directly. TanStack Table features expose dedicated APIs for interacting with their state, and those APIs are the safest way to make changes because they preserve the feature's own rules and related updates.\n\nFor example, use pagination APIs instead of mutating pagination state yourself:\n\n```tsx\ntable.nextPage()\ntable.previousPage()\ntable.setPageIndex(0)\ntable.setPageSize(25)\n```\n\nThe same idea applies across features. Use APIs like `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setColumnFilters(...)`, `column.toggleVisibility()`, or `row.toggleSelected()` instead of manually editing the underlying state object.\n\nIf you only care about setting starting values, use `initialState`. If you want to reset a state slice back to its initial value, use that feature's reset API.\n\nIf you really do need to write a state slice directly, the low-level write surface for internally owned state is the matching base atom:\n\n```tsx\ntable.baseAtoms.pagination.set((old) => ({\n  ...old,\n  pageIndex: 0,\n}))\n```\n\nDirect base atom writes should be rare. If a slice is owned by an external atom passed through `atoms`, write to that external atom instead; `table.atoms.pagination` will read from the external atom, not the internal base atom.\n\n### Custom Initial State\n\nIf you only need to customize the starting value for some table state, use `initialState`. You still do not need to manage that state yourself.\n\n`initialState` only applies to registered state slices. It is used to create the table's initial state and is also used by reset APIs such as `table.resetSorting()` or `table.resetPagination()`. Changing the `initialState` object later does not reset table state.\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  initialState: {\n    sorting: [\n      {\n        id: 'age',\n        desc: true,\n      },\n    ],\n    pagination: {\n      pageIndex: 0,\n      pageSize: 25,\n    },\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not provide the same state slice in multiple ownership places unless you intentionally want one to win. For a slice like `pagination`, prefer exactly one of `initialState.pagination`, `atoms.pagination`, or `state.pagination` as the source of truth. External atoms take precedence over external `state`; external `state` syncs into the table's internal base atom.\n\n#### Resetting to Initial State\n\nFeature reset APIs reset to `table.initialState` by default. Many reset APIs also accept `true` to reset to that feature's blank/default state instead:\n\n```tsx\ntable.resetSorting()\ntable.resetPagination()\ntable.resetPagination(true)\n```\n\nSlice reset APIs like `resetPagination()` update through that feature's state updater and can update an externally owned atom. The core `table.reset()` API resets the internal base atoms, so do not use it as the primary way to reset state that is owned by external atoms.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nIf you need easy access to table state in other parts of your application, you can control individual state slices. In v9, external atoms are the recommended way to do this because they preserve the atomic state model and let Octane subscribe to exactly the slices it needs.\n\n#### External Atoms\n\nUse external atoms when the app should own one or more table state slices. Create stable writable atoms with `useCreateAtom` from TanStack Store, pass them to the table's `atoms` option, and subscribe to them with `useSelector` anywhere else in your app.\n\nThis is especially useful for server-side data fetching. Pagination, sorting, or filters often belong in a query key, and external atoms let the app and the table share those values without lifting the entire table state through Octane state.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'octane'\nimport {\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  // no paginatedRowModel: manual server-side pagination does not need it\n})\n\nfunction App() @{\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const [result, setResult] = useState({ rows: [], rowCount: 0 })\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    let cancelled = false\n    fetchData(pagination).then((nextResult) => {\n      if (!cancelled) setResult(nextResult)\n    })\n    return () => {\n      cancelled = true\n    }\n  }, [pagination])\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data: result.rows,\n    rowCount: result.rowCount,\n    atoms: {\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n    manualPagination: true,\n  })\n\n  // table pagination APIs update paginationAtom\n}\n```\n\nWhen using the `atoms` option for a slice, you do not need to add the matching `on[State]Change` option. For example, if you pass `atoms.pagination`, table pagination APIs update that atom directly.\n\n#### External State\n\nThe classic `state` plus `on[State]Change` pattern is still supported. This can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. Octane state updates re-render the owner component, and that render cannot be avoided by the `useTable` selector in the same way atom subscriptions can be placed lower in the tree.\n\nTo control a slice with external state, pass both the state value and the matching callback:\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})\n```\n\nThe v8-style `onStateChange` option (a single global state callback) is gone in v9. It is not supported by `useTable`. Use per-slice `on[State]Change` callbacks paired with `state.<slice>`, or external atoms via the `atoms` option. If you truly need to observe every state change, subscribe to `table.store` directly.\n\n#### Render and Commit Timing\n\nDuring an Octane render, `useTable` stages the newest options immediately, so table reads in that same render see fresh data, columns, callbacks, and controlled state. It does not publish that controlled state to `table.store` while the component is rendering.\n\nAfter Octane accepts the render, a layout effect marks its snapshot committed and publishes the captured controlled state. Suspended or abandoned renders therefore cannot leak speculative state. The owner drops the matching post-commit notification because it has already rendered that snapshot, while independently mounted `table.Subscribe` consumers still receive the update before paint.\n\nThis commit behavior applies to controlled `state` options. External atoms remain the direct source of truth and table APIs write to them synchronously.\n\n##### On State Change Callbacks\n\nThe `on[State]Change` callbacks are useful when you are controlling a matching slice through the `state` option. They work like Octane state setters: an updater can be a raw value or a function that receives the previous value and returns the next value.\n\nIf you provide an `on[State]Change` callback, also provide the corresponding value in `state`. For example, `onSortingChange` should be paired with `state.sorting`.\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n})\n```\n\nIf you need to add logic inside a callback, check whether the incoming updater is a function or a value:\n\n```tsx\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onPaginationChange: (updater) => {\n    setPagination((old) => {\n      const next = updater instanceof Function ? updater(old) : updater\n\n      // side effects or validation can happen here\n\n      return next\n    })\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### State Types\n\nMost complex states in TanStack Table have their own TypeScript types that you can import and use. This is useful for Octane state, external atoms, and helper functions that work with table state.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  type PaginationState,\n  type RowSelectionState,\n  type SortingState,\n  type TableState,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([\n  {\n    id: 'age',\n    desc: true,\n  },\n])\n```\n\n`TableState<typeof features>` is inferred from the features registered on that table:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n})\n\ntype MyTableState = TableState<typeof features>\n```\n\nPrefer feature-specific state types like `SortingState`, `PaginationState`, or `RowSelectionState` when you are creating local state or external atoms for one slice.\n"}
